var e={meta:{slug:"angular-material-to-primeng",title:"Migrating a Large Angular App from Material to PrimeNG (Without a Big-Bang Rewrite)",excerpt:"How we moved a multi-module Angular workspace off Angular Material and onto PrimeNG one screen at a time, while the product kept shipping.",tag:"Angular",readTime:"7 min",publishedLabel:"Field notes"},intro:"Every UI library migration guide I'd read assumed you could stop the world: freeze the roadmap, swap the library, fix what breaks, ship. That's not how it went on Compito, a multi-tenant practice-management platform where the business kept requesting new modules the entire time we were mid-migration. Here's what actually worked.",sections:[{heading:"Why leave Material in the first place",paragraphs:["Angular Material is a solid, accessible component set, but it's opinionated about density and theming in ways that fought us constantly on data-heavy enterprise screens — dense tables, inline editing, compact toolbars. PrimeNG's Aura theme gave us the same accessibility guarantees with a component set built for exactly that kind of dashboard-and-table-heavy product.","The other driver was velocity. PrimeNG's table, dialog and form components come with far more built-in behavior — filtering, sorting, virtual scroll, templated cells — than Material's, which meant less custom wrapper code per screen once the migration landed."]},{heading:"Rule one: no big-bang rewrite",paragraphs:["A full rewrite means a long-lived branch, a merge nightmare, and a product that can't take new features until it's done. Instead we migrated module by module, screen by screen, inside the same running application. A screen either used Material or PrimeNG — never both mixed on the same page, since that's where visual inconsistency creeps in fastest.","This meant living with two component libraries in the bundle for months. That's a real cost — bigger bundle, two theme systems to reason about — but it's a cost you can measure and schedule around, unlike 'the migration branch has been open for 11 weeks and nobody remembers what's in it.'"],bullets:["Migrate leaf screens first — the ones nothing else depends on — to build confidence before touching shared layout shells","Keep a single source of truth for theme tokens (spacing, radius, color) so Material and PrimeNG screens don't visually clash mid-migration","Track migration status per screen in a simple checklist, not tribal knowledge"]},{heading:"The theming layer is where the real work is",paragraphs:["The component swap itself is mechanical — find every mat-table, replace with p-table, adjust the API surface. The part that actually takes engineering judgment is theming. We rebuilt the stylesheet on a 7-1 SCSS architecture and configured PrimeUIX theme presets so PrimeNG components picked up the same design tokens Material screens were still using, rather than shipping two visually distinct products under one login.","We also used this migration as the forcing function to finally eliminate ::ng-deep across the codebase — every override that had accumulated over years of 'just deep-select into Material's internals to fix this one spacing issue' had to be re-solved properly using PrimeNG's documented theming API instead."]},{heading:"What I'd tell someone starting this today",paragraphs:["Don't estimate the migration by counting components. Estimate it by counting screens, because the real cost is verifying each screen's behavior — sort order, empty states, loading states, error states — survived the swap, not just that it visually looks right.","And keep the old library's screens fully functional the entire time. The moment you break something in the 'not yet migrated' half of the app to make the 'migrated' half easier, you've turned a phased migration into a big-bang one with extra steps."]}],takeaway:"A phased UI-library migration is a scheduling problem as much as a technical one — the mechanical component swaps are the easy part; protecting a shippable product for months while two design systems coexist is the actual work."};export{e as angularMaterialToPrimeng};
